# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository, always.
#
# This is not cosmetic. `vendor/manager.js`, `store.js` and `tools.js` are
# byte-for-byte copies of upstream whose sha256 is recorded in
# `vendor/PROVENANCE.md` (and re-checked by anyone re-syncing with
# starxxy/dsh-ssh). Those hashes are hashes of LF content. With the Windows
# default `core.autocrlf=true` and no attributes file, git would store CRLF,
# and a fresh clone on any machine would produce different bytes than the
# recorded hashes — the exact failure mode PROVENANCE.md warns about.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on the way in and checks out LF on every
# platform, so the hashes hold everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows launchers are the one exception: they are parsed by cmd.exe and
# Windows Script Host, and a `.cmd` whose lines end in a bare LF can mis-parse
# its own `goto` labels. These check out as CRLF — and because the files are
# GBK-encoded (cmd reads them with the system ANSI code page), `text` also
# keeps git from guessing wrong about them.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# Binary files must never be line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
